# Cloudflare Pages response headers.
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/headers/
#
# The site publishes AI-agent-facing plain-markdown mirrors of its pages
# (<page-url>index.md), a curated /llms.txt index and a concatenated
# /llms-full.txt. They intentionally duplicate the canonical HTML content, so
# keep them out of search-engine indexes (avoiding duplicate-content issues)
# while leaving them fully fetchable by AI crawlers and agents — robots.txt
# allows them, noindex only stops SERP indexing, not retrieval.


# ---- caching -------------------------------------------------------------
# Fonts are the one asset class safe to cache forever unasked: the filenames come
# from @fontsource and encode family, subset and weight, so a given name never
# changes content. Cloudflare's default (max-age=14400) had the browser
# revalidating ~146 KB of immutable woff2 every four hours.
/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# /css/* and /js/* are now content-hashed at build time (base.css ->
# base.<hash>.css, see scripts/lib/asset-manifest.js), which is the prerequisite
# this comment used to ask for. A changed file is a new URL, so `immutable` cannot
# pin stale bytes — the previous max-age=14400 on unfingerprinted names could, and
# did: on 2026-07-30 a deployed CSS fix kept being served from the old edge copy
# for hours while the canonical URL looked fine.
#
# Safe as a wildcard specifically because the build emits NO unhashed copy. If you
# ever reintroduce a plain /css/<name>.css, this header would cache it for a year.
/css/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/js/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# robots.txt and the agent-facing index files must be CHEAP TO CORRECT.
#
# Measured live on both zones 2026-08-03:
#
#   cache-control: public, max-age=14400, must-revalidate
#   cf-cache-status: REVALIDATED
#
# That is Cloudflare Pages' default, not ours — the string 14400 appears in this
# repo only in the two comments above, and no zone Cache Rule is involved (checked:
# the built _headers had no /robots.txt entry at all). The consequence is that a
# wrong Disallow, a missing Allow for a new AI crawler, or a corrected Sitemap line
# takes FOUR HOURS to reach the crawler that needs it — and robots.txt is the one
# file on the site where being wrong for four hours can cost a crawl of everything.
#
# Same reasoning for /llms.txt, /llms-full.txt and the mirrors: they are the
# ingestion surface, they are regenerated on every deploy, and an agent holding a
# four-hour-old copy of the index is following links to content that may have moved.
# `must-revalidate` with max-age=0 keeps the edge copy and the ETag — the 304s
# already work — while making every fetch ask first.
#
# One block per path rather than two, so this does not depend on Cloudflare merging
# rules that match the same URL — the noindex and the Cache-Control belong to the
# same decision anyway.
#
# VERIFIED LIVE 2026-08-03, after deploying this file. It works for llms.txt,
# llms-full.txt and all 1,234 mirrors — and does NOT work for /robots.txt:
#
#   /css/fonts.<hash>.css   max-age=31536000, immutable   <- rule applied
#   /llms.txt               max-age=0, must-revalidate     <- rule applied
#   /index.md               max-age=0, must-revalidate     <- rule applied
#   /sitemap.xml            max-age=0, must-revalidate     <- no rule; XML default
#   /favicon.svg            max-age=14400, must-revalidate <- no rule; asset default
#   /robots.txt             max-age=14400, must-revalidate <- RULE IGNORED
#
# Ruled out, each by probe: a syntax error (the built file is well-formed, and every
# neighbouring rule in it works), a stale edge entry (`?cb=` forces
# cf-cache-status: MISS and still returns 14400), and Cloudflare rewriting the file
# (the live body is byte-identical to _site-org/robots.txt). What is left is that
# Cloudflare Pages does not apply _headers to /robots.txt — it appears to own that
# path the way it owns _headers, _redirects and _routes.json.
#
# The rule is KEPT rather than deleted: it is correct, it costs nothing, and it will
# start working if Pages ever stops special-casing the path. But it does not work
# today, so the only lever that would is a zone-level Cache Rule (Caching -> Cache
# Rules, match `http.request.uri.path eq "/robots.txt"`, Browser TTL 1 minute) —
# a dashboard action, not a repo one. Until then robots.txt takes up to four hours
# to propagate, which is a known cost rather than a mystery.
# check-geo-live.js reports it as a WARNING for exactly that reason, while a
# regression on llms.txt stays a hard failure.
/robots.txt
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/llms.txt
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/llms-full.txt
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/*.md
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

{%- comment -%}
The search corpus (scripts/gen-search-index.js). Same treatment as llms.txt and for
the same two reasons: it is regenerated on every deploy, and it is not a page.

`max-age=0, must-revalidate` rather than the year-long immutable that /js/* gets,
because these are NOT content-hashed — the filenames are stable so js/search.js can
fetch them without being told a hash. Revalidation is a conditional request that
comes back 304 with no body, which is the right trade for a file whose whole job is
to describe the current state of the site: an index cached for four hours points
search results at content that may have moved, and there is no way to bust it.

noindex because these are the same content as the pages they index, in a form built
for a fetch() rather than for a reader — exactly the duplicate-content argument that
put the header on the markdown mirrors.
{%- endcomment %}
# There is deliberately NO Access-Control-Allow-Origin here. Cross-site search reads the
# OTHER edition's index from `/search-peer-*.json` on THIS origin — copied in at build
# time by scripts/copy-peer-index.js — so no cross-origin request is ever made and no
# header has to permit one. That file explains why copying beat both CORS and a symlink.
/search-index.json
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/search-text.json
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# Anchor -> line range into each page's markdown mirror. Same treatment for the same reason
# it applies to the mirrors themselves: it addresses them by LINE NUMBER, so a stale copy
# does not merely point somewhere out of date, it slices the wrong text out of a page that
# has since changed — and reports no error while doing it.
/search-sections.json
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# The peer copies get identical treatment: regenerated every deploy, not a page, and
# stale-cached they would point search at content that has moved.
/search-peer-index.json
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/search-peer-text.json
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

{%- if edition == "org" %}
# The package status feed. Same revalidate-always treatment as the search corpus,
# and for a sharper version of the same reason: this file states what version each
# package IS. A copy cached for four hours does not merely go out of date, it hands
# an agent a version number the registry no longer agrees with — and an agent that
# fetched it from the project's own domain has no reason to doubt it. `generated`
# inside the file is the second line of defence, for a copy that got cached anyway.
#
# noindex because /status/ is the page; this is the same facts shaped for a fetch().
/status.json
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
{%- endif %}

# /search/ is a results page: unbounded URL space (`?q=` takes anything), no content of
# its own, and every link on it exists on a real page. The meta tag says the same thing —
# both, because a header covers the case where a crawler reads only the response.
#
# Deliberately NOT a robots.txt Disallow: that would stop the page being fetched, and a
# page that is never fetched is a page whose noindex is never read.
/search/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow

# ---- security ------------------------------------------------------------
# Not ranking factors, but cheap and worth having. Left out on purpose:
#   * Strict-Transport-Security — a year-long max-age is effectively
#     irreversible, and it is a deployment decision rather than an SEO one.
#     Enable it once you are certain every subdomain (mcp.imqueue.org included)
#     is HTTPS-only:  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000
#   * Content-Security-Policy — this site runs inline scripts (the no-FOUC theme
#     init) plus GA4 and Clarity, so a policy needs to be authored and tested
#     against real pages rather than pasted in.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
